  "query_preview": "../Monday through Friday/...\nMonday, a surface high pressure will be dominant along the coast,\nserving as the catalyst for continued sea breezes for our region;\nnorthwest moderate to fresh breezes for our coastal waters and \nstrong breezes impacting localized areas in the inner channels \n(Taiya, N/Lynn Canal to name a few). Monday night a low positions \nitself in the NW Gulf bringing split flow to the coast: southeast \nwinds from Fairweather Grounds to Cape St. Elias and continued \nnorth-westerlies south of Cape Edgecumbe. This system will be \nresponsible for bringing widespread rain to areas along and west \nof Icy Strait, to Prince William Sound, further detail on rain at \nthe end. For areas south of the majestic Admiralty Island, \nexpecting some cloudiness but we will likely see an extension of \nsummer conditions... temperatures in their seasonal normals and 20\npercent or less chances for rain. One of a precious few remaining\nopportunities to feel the sun before we submit to the winter\u2019s \ndarkness. \n\nTouching on the rain potential, at this time most guidance \nsolutions point to light rainfall for next week; however, there \nare some outliers picking up on much higher IVT magnitudes from an\nexcellent fetch of moisture across the N/Pac, which would \ntranslate to increased chances for more widespread rain. This \nuncertainty is based off additional moisture transport from \ntropical storms/hurricanes in the central Pac. For now, we message\nlighter rain for the north as this moisture plume is sheared \napart Monday into the midweek.",
